INT. MINIVAN - TOLL PLAZA - DAY

Crammed. Juice boxes. A dashboard phone holder with a call in progress.

GERALD OKONJO-HART, 40s, grips the wheel. SUNITA, 40s, wedges a map between her knees. In the back, GUS, 10, kicks the seat. PIPER, 14, stares at her phone.

GRANDMA BERNADETTE (V.O.)
(over speaker)
Are you there yet? Dinner's getting cold.

GERALD
We're at the toll, Mom.

GUS
Mom, tell Piper to stop breathing on me.

PIPER
I'm not even close.

SUNITA
Both of you. Quiet.

At the booth, a TOLL ATTENDANT, 20s, leans out. He eyes the van's tangle of wires and limbs.

ATTENDANT
Four fifty.

Gerald pats his pocket. Pats the other. Pats the dashboard.

GERALD
Sunita. Wallet?

SUNITA
You had it.

GERALD
I gave it to you.

SUNITA
You gave me nothing.

GRANDMA BERNADETTE (V.O.)
Is that a toll? Gerald, I told you to take the back road!

The van behind them honks. Then another. Gus presses the window button. Dollar bills and napkins fly out. The attendant snatches one.

ATTENDANT
That's a coupon for sandwiches.

GUS
Is it good?

ATTENDANT
Buy one get one.

He waves them through. Gerald stares at him. The bar lifts.

GRANDMA BERNADETTE (V.O.)
Did you pay?

GERALD
(driving away)
We did, Mom. Sort of.
